["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"aep","stream_identity":"01a0d3bf-067c-7068-af65-bd964ce78fcb","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"story","fields":{"body":"# Story: A workspace names its member repositories, and pins them\n\n## Outcome\n\nSomebody can point one command at a set of repositories and get the same answer on their laptop and\nin CI — because each member is named with a locator that cannot mean two different trees.\n\n## Context\n\nNothing today says which repositories a question should be answered over. A project file says what\n*this* repository runs under; there is no file that says what *several* repositories are. The\nlocator problem is already solved once, by `project.yaml`'s `protocols:`, and reusing that type\ncarries its two refusals with it for the same reasons.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`.engineering/workspace.yaml` at `version: aep.workspace/1` with `members: [{name, source}]`;\n`source` takes the same `ProtocolSource` locator, so an **absolute path is refused** — true on one\nmachine and false in CI — and an **unpinned git locator is refused**, because a tree that can move\nunder you is a dependency whose meaning changes with no commit in your repository; a pinned\n`git+ssh://…#<40-hex>` is accepted; a generated JSON Schema is committed and checked; and the type\nresolves nothing — a member nobody has checked out is a normal condition, not a broken workspace.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nFetching or cloning a member. The manifest declares; the shell resolves. Nothing here reaches the\nnetwork.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"ent-f7b03f6dd13094578b6bfecc7cb9c5cc","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/workspace-manifest","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":4,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"# Story: A workspace names its member repositories, and pins them\n\n## Outcome\n\nSomebody can point one command at a set of repositories and get the same answer on their laptop and\nin CI — because each member is named with a locator that cannot mean two different trees.\n\n## Context\n\nNothing today says which repositories a question should be answered over. A project file says what\n*this* repository runs under; there is no file that says what *several* repositories are. The\nlocator problem is already solved once, by `project.yaml`'s `protocols:`, and reusing that type\ncarries its two refusals with it for the same reasons.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`.engineering/workspace.yaml` at `version: aep.workspace/1` with `members: [{name, source}]`;\n`source` takes the same `ProtocolSource` locator, so an **absolute path is refused** — true on one\nmachine and false in CI — and an **unpinned git locator is refused**, because a tree that can move\nunder you is a dependency whose meaning changes with no commit in your repository; a pinned\n`git+ssh://…#<40-hex>` is accepted; a generated JSON Schema is committed and checked; and the type\nresolves nothing — a member nobody has checked out is a normal condition, not a broken workspace.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nFetching or cloning a member. The manifest declares; the shell resolves. Nothing here reaches the\nnetwork.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:one-cli-many-repositories"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"One file naming the members and where each store is: a path, or a pinned git+...#<40-hex> locator. The pin is not decoration - a governing tree that can move under you is a dependency whose meaning changes with no commit in your repository.","title":"A workspace names its member repositories, and pins them"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"},"relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:one-cli-many-repositories"}],"summary":"One file naming the members and where each store is: a path, or a pinned git+...#<40-hex> locator. The pin is not decoration - a governing tree that can move under you is a dependency whose meaning changes with no commit in your repository.","title":"A workspace names its member repositories, and pins them"},"id":"ent-f7b03f6dd13094578b6bfecc7cb9c5cc","lifecycle_state":"implemented","revision":4,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["story","ent-f7b03f6dd13094578b6bfecc7cb9c5cc"]}]